 #:seon.task{:name "refusal-html-reads-as-fragments"
             :plan "Since ea802169d each shown value in a refusal's HTML is a block seon-data-panel mid-sentence, breaking the sentence; the sentence repeats the schema key after 'a string'; a missing key shows :seon.error/unknown as the offending value; the refusal path shows a raw entity id [56626 :my.plan.item/title]; a fault with no operation renders an empty seon-kicker paragraph. Values inline (a CSS rule for .seon-error-problem .seon-data-panel in resources/public/css/input.css), no repeated key, a missing key named as missing, no entity-id dump, no empty kicker; check browser paint (fix-html-views 1c10d3536). Read `seon.error/render-html` and `docs/research/agent-platform/red-triage-3-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "health-stays-faulted-on-dead-process-faults"
             :plan "At 18:01Z 2026-09-27 all 10 open faults came from dead processes and 7 were stale: resolution (error.clj ~1309-1354) fires only when the digest of `:seon.instrument/fn` changes, and that fn is often the reporter (memory-pressure-crossed!, record-write-refusal-fault!, `seon.maintenance/exception-value`) or an outer wrapper (`seon.test/run`'s digest change resolved three record-tx signatures while record-tx still refused; settle-batch-refusal! stays open after its cause was fixed elsewhere), or absent (superv.async; see seon.error/a-deleted-functions-fault-never-resolves). runtime_status reads :faulted whenever any fault is open. Let the producer's error schema declare process scope (e.g. memory pressure, the write-refusal park, a stop's interrupt, `:seon.flow/launcher-stopped`) and resolve such a fault once none of its occurrences' processes is live; resolve by the failing operation (`:seon.error/operation`), not the outermost armed Var. Done when a regression's process-scoped fault reads resolved after its process ends, and editing an unrelated wrapper resolves nothing. Read `seon.error/resolution`, `seon.fault/fault!`, `seon.problems/open-faults` and `docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-fault-notification-is-its-errors-edn-as-text"
             :plan "A fault's notification message carries the error value printed as EDN text (message-tx: `:seon.message/content` (`seon.render.value/bounded-text` (value fact))). On default 2026-09-27, 33 of root's 35 unread messages were such strings, so its inbox (and the runtime trigger it wakes on) shows \"#:seon.error{:at\"…+412: the content head is timestamp and layer, never what failed. Content is for the reader: the error's message and operation as its text, with the whole record one read away through `:seon.message/about` (the signature). Done when a notified agent's inbox entry reads what failed and where within its first line, and the error value is not re-printed into the message. Read `seon.error/message-tx`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-distance-zero-fault-render-omits-whether-it-is-open"
             :plan "A fault named in a function's related data (:seon.instrument/_fn) shows its message and occurrence count but not whether it is open or resolved (`seon.error/resolution`), so thirteen faults beside `seon.test/run` cannot be told apart from history (2026-09-28). Done when the distance-zero fault render carries its resolution. Read `seon.error/render-ai` and `docs/research/agent-platform/profiling-and-related-data-2026-09-28.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-error-observation-pull-names-541-attributes"
             :plan "`seon.error/observation-selector` names 541 attributes (every declared error extension's members), so pulling 47 error signatures costs 133 ms against 4.6 ms for the wildcard pull it replaced in `seon.problems/error-signatures` (default, armed, 2026-09-28); the wildcard read depended on every attribute, so the selector is kept for attribution. Done when an error pull costs about the wildcard's while its reads still name attributes (e.g. errors store refs and a blob, plan §3). Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/health-page--RESUME.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-fixed-fault-with-an-answering-task-has-no-proof-path"
             :plan "Fault 7b2cf983e1a4 (unreadable-file!, fixed by 9516842e1 + 4b29a0689 on 2026-09-28) stayed open 8 days; 13 faults open 2026-10-06. `seon.error/resolution` closes a fault whose producer digest changed ONLY when no task answers it (empty? tasks); with an answering task (every producer-task auto-created one) it needs `seon.error/proof`: a test naming the task, red on a proposing branch and green in one accepted merge proposal. A fix landed by bin/lane commit never records that, so the four faults with a changed producer digest (`my.fs/read`, `my.fs/write!`, `my.shell/run!`, `my.program/diff`) are held open by their own task, and a fault whose producer is the thrower (unreadable-file!, `seon.db/transact!`, retire-branch!) never sees a digest change when the cause is a caller or the loader (then == now for 7b2cf9). Decide what counts as proof of a fix outside the proposal flow (options in docs/research or the agent report: a green stored run of a test naming the task at current digests; a human resolve!; resolve by occurrence process scope), then derive it in `seon.error/resolution` without a second mechanism. Done when a regression writes a fault with a task, changes the producer, records the proof and reads the fault resolved, and the 7b2cf9 case resolves. Read `seon.problems/open-faults` and `seon.task/resolution`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "s1-left-metadata-readers-and-stamps-in-three-test-files"
             :plan "`seon.error.declared-identity-test/producers-declare-the-identity-settlement-records` now passes, but fixtures in `seon.cluster.turn-test` still stamp `:seon.error/declared-schema` metadata. Convert those producers to ordinary `:seon.error/type` data so `seon.error/recording` and `seon.turn/record-attempt!` preserve the named error and its `:seon.ai/exhausted-finish-reason`. The remaining proof is `seon.cluster.turn-test/reasoning-starvation-persists-usage-finish-and-the-named-error`, which must assert the stored variant and finish reason. Remove the remaining metadata readers and stamps; a green declared-identity test alone does not complete this task. Read `seon.render/declared-producers`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/model-trial--review-s1-trial-astra-s1.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/model-trial--review-s1-trial-sol61-s1.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "error-messages-print-growing-values"
             :plan "Error messages are built by printing values that grow with input (the offending value, every argument, a whole entity or transaction report, every moved identity, ex-message chains copied into new ex-info messages), so one refusal can carry megabytes of Seon's own text. Owner 2026-10-07: never print bulk in errors; the error map holds the value by reference (offending digest or blob) and the message names it in bounded words, the value printer shows the rest. Inventory 2026-10-07, class error-text in tmp/no-print-hash/print-sites.tsv, 56 sites: db.clj 9, fn.clj 5, schema/internal.cljc 4, test/seon/test_support.clj 4, config.clj 3, schema.clj 3, schema/admission.clj 3, test/runner.clj 3, turn.clj 3, cluster/source.clj 2, error.clj 2, render.clj 2, render/transcript.clj 2, script/dev/harness_costs.clj 1, script/operator.clj 1, batch.clj 1, cluster/agent.clj 1, effect.clj 1, fault.clj 1, problems.clj 1, sci/arm.clj 1, sci/kernel.clj 1, test.clj 1, turn/batch.clj 1. Done when no message interpolates a collection or whole value (scalars only), each site keeps its value as data, and a regression in seon.error-test refuses a write whose offending value prints over 10 MB and asserts the message is under 200 estimated tokens while :seon.error/offending-digest names the value. Read `seon.db/value-type-error`, `seon.error/recording`, `seon.fn/require-committed!`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--notes.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--print-sites.tsv`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "error-admission-is-a-second-clipper"
             :plan "seon.error (bounded-admission, bounded-error-admission, bounded-text, bound-text, fit-fact-payload), seon.error.refusal (bounded-cost, bounded-data, chain) and seon.edit (bounded-values) each bound and clip values by their own byte caps, printing values to measure them (pr-str per fact, utf8-size of printed text): a second and third clipping system beside the value printer. Owner 2026-10-07: no random clipping in a dozen spots; real values are kept and the value printer is the only way we print. Inventory class second-clipper, 36 sites: error.clj 22, `seon.error/refusal`.clj 9, edit.clj 5. Done when errors store the whole value by reference (blob digest) with no clip, the printer alone bounds what is shown, the three bounding walks are deleted, and seon.error-test asserts an error over a 10 MB value stores its digest and the reply shows it through the printer's elision with a dig form. Read `seon.edit/form`, `seon.error.refusal/chain`, `seon.error/fit-fact-payload`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--notes.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/no-print-hash--print-sites.tsv`."}
