 #:seon.task{:name "one-signature-covers-different-install-failures"
             :plan "`seon.error/signature` hashes layer, operation, declared schema, throwable class, frame site, expected key or shape, location path and `:seon.error/producer`, but never the refused function or its cause. On default (JVM pid 2041, HEAD 984789a27, 2026-10-14, agents dd-probe-1 to dd-probe-8) the signature `7c52d6ce256964e2…` is shared by \\\"Cannot interpret probe.dd1/uses-k1: … k1\\\", \\\"Cannot interpret probe.dd2/measure: … request\\\" and \\\"Cannot interpret probe.dd8/f1: … x/upper-case\\\" on three branches, so one occurrence count (`:seon.error.occurrence/count`) covers unrelated defects and a fix to one resolves the signature for the others. These refusals are built by `seon.sci.eval/interpretation-error`, which carries the function symbol. Done when two install refusals for different functions or different causes have different `:seon.error/signature` values, by carrying the refused function symbol in the observation the producer identity reads, and the same refusal repeated still yields one signature. Proof: to write before assignment. Read `seon.error/signature`, `seon.sci.eval/interpretation-error` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/digest-and-def-defects.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-unresolved-alias-name-offers-its-nearest-core-name"
             :plan "An agent wrote `(str/subs \"#the-goal\" 1)` and the reply was \"Unable to resolve symbol: str/subs\" with no suggestion of `clojure.core/subs`; it retried six times (Implement attempt of live chain rough run 1, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/live-chain-rough-1.md`). The same error for `seon.agent/id` carried a `:seon.error/nearest` list. Done when an unresolved symbol under a resolvable alias (`str/` for `clojure.string`) whose simple name is declared in `clojure.core` or in another namespace the agent has loaded answers that name in `:seon.error/nearest` (`clojure.core/subs`), proven by a deftest over the SCI evaluation of `(str/subs \"abc\" 1)`. Fix at the producer of the nearest list: `seon.error/nearest-names` over the names of the alias target, widened to the simple name's other owners. Read `seon.error/nearest-names` and `seon.sci.eval/interpretation-error`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-unresolved-symbol-reply-searches-every-declaration"
             :plan "An eval request with an unresolved symbol cost 97 ms and 84 MB on default (2026-10-17, MCP `eval_clj` on an agent branch), almost all of it the nearest-name search: `seon.error/missing-symbol` finds the name and `seon.error/nearest-names` (reached from `seon.error/reply-value`) tokenizes and scores every public declaration, with `me/levenshtein` over each, on every such request. The candidate list and its word frequencies depend only on the program's declared names, not on the guessed name, so derive them once per program content (a `seon.cache` kind keyed by the digest of the declared symbol set) and score only the wanted name against it; limit the Levenshtein pass to candidates that share a word. Done when the unresolved-symbol request costs under 20 ms and 10 MB warm, asserted through `seon.profile/measure` in the new deftest `seon.error-test/an-unresolved-symbol-reply-costs-under-twenty-milliseconds` (written by a later lane; red on HEAD: 97 ms / 84 MB). Related, not the same: the task an-unresolved-alias-name-offers-its-nearest-core-name is about the answer's content. Read `seon.error/nearest-names`, `seon.error/reply-value` and `seon.cache`."
             :proofs #{seon.error-test/an-unresolved-symbol-reply-costs-under-twenty-milliseconds}}
]
